`context/instructions/conversation.md` is the contract for the register the agent
uses when it talks to the user, as distinct from the register its documents use.
It is portable: it names the two registers and their readers, not a tool. Read it
with `context/instructions/writing.md` for the document register and
`context/instructions/authoring.md` for how guidance is shaped.

**When this applies.** A turn opens or closes a unit of work, or asks the user to
decide something. A mid-work progress line and a one-word acknowledgement stay
terse: this register is for the turns where the reader has to understand and
choose, and a turn that asks for nothing and reports nothing is not one.

## Two registers, two readers

| Register | Reader | Contract | Owner |
|---|---|---|---|
| Document | works in the corpus, now or later | technical, terse, every reference named by path | `context/instructions/writing.md` |
| User-facing | decides what happens next | plain, contextual, carries the choice | this file |

The registers answer different questions. "Is this claim supported?" is asked by a
document reader; "what happens if I say yes?" is asked by the person the work is
for. A sentence that is interpretive filler in a plan can be the payload of a
decision turn.

## The four-part turn

When the reader must decide, the turn carries four things and one question:

1. **What happened** — the outcome, in the user's terms, with the number or path
   that makes it checkable.
2. **What it means for the work** — the consequence for what comes next, not a
   restatement of the first part.
3. **The choice** — the options that actually exist, each with its cost; if there
   is one option, say so instead of staging a choice.
4. **The recommendation** — which one you would take and why, in one line.

Then **exactly one question**, naming the decision. A second question makes the
first unanswerable; a batch of findings is one report with one decision.

The parts are a checklist, not a form to fill verbatim. A turn that carries all
five and reads like a template has failed differently.

## A worked pair

The same work, in the wrong register and then the right one.

**Mid-work, wrong** — a decision turn written as a status line:

> Phase 2 is done. Tests pass. Next: Phase 3.

The reader cannot tell whether anything is asked of them, what saying nothing
would mean, or which way the work is about to go.

**Decision turn, right**:

> The retry now stops after three attempts instead of ten, so a failing call gives
> up in about two seconds rather than seven. The remaining change is the sentence
> that tells the caller why.
> One thing to settle: the fourth failure currently surfaces as a timeout.
> Reporting it as "gave up after three attempts" is truthful and changes the error
> text some callers log; keeping "timeout" preserves the current contract but
> misdescribes what happened.
> I would report what happened and accept the log change.
> Do you want the new wording?

**A mid-work line, right** — terse is still right when nothing is being decided:

> Phase 2 done, tests pass. Phase 3 next.

The pair is the test: if the reader can answer the question only after asking a
follow-up, the turn was in the wrong register.

## Precedence with `writing.md`

- The document register never governs a chat turn. Its cut-list removes
  interpretive metadiscourse from a document; in a decision turn that same
  sentence may be the explanation the reader needs.
- The user-facing register never governs a document. A plan stays technical, and
  the explanation that belongs in the turn does not move into the file.
- A mid-work line, a progress note and a one-word answer are neither: they stay
  short, and the brevity rules of the code-work contract still apply.

When the two rules point in opposite directions, the artefact decides the
register: a document being edited is the document register; a message being sent
is the user-facing one.

## Precision survives translation

Plain does not mean vague. Keep:

- the number that makes a claim checkable ("3 warnings, unchanged", not "a few");
- a path or a command the reader may act on, quoted exactly;
- an exact term when the decision turns on it, with a one-line gloss the first
  time;
- a risk, a fact or a hedge that carries real uncertainty.

Translate or drop an internal name that only labels a mechanism: "the function
that resolves the project root" is better than its identifier, unless the reader
is about to edit that function.

## Simplicity is not vagueness

A rule that asks for context can drift into length. The guard is the question,
not the paragraph count: if the reader can decide from the message, it is done; if
they cannot, more prose is not the fix — the missing part is one of the four.

Over-correction is its own failure. Trimming a fact, a risk or a hedge to make a
turn shorter is the same defect as padding it (`context/instructions/writing.md`,
*Over-correction is its own failure*).

## Language

The answer is in the user's language, including when the work, the documents and
the code are in English. A document keeps the project's language; a quotation
stays in the language of its source. A project that declares another answer
language overrides this paragraph.

## What nothing checks

No gate reads a turn, so the rule binds only through this wording. The first
message that leaves the reader asking for the context they needed is the evidence
to revisit it: record it, and fix the wording rather than the reader's habit.
